// ── RE-HOMED fail/nll -> pass/nll BY THE #92 CONST-PROMOTION DECISION ──────
// This fixture asserted that `fn make() -> &i32 { return &5i32; }` is
// REFUSED. rustc ACCEPTS it: `&5i32` is a borrow of a constant expression and
// is promoted to `&'static i32` (RFC 1414 rvalue static promotion), so the
// referent is in read-only static storage and there is no temporary to die.
// The old note below described B75's prov_of recursion, which is correct for
// `&<call result>` and `&<struct lit>` and WRONG for a constant literal — the
// refusal it pinned was a DIVERGENCE from rustc, not conformance to it.
//
// NOTHING WAS WEAKENED AND NOTHING WAS DELETED. The same program is still
// tested, and the claim about it is now STRICTLY STRONGER: `fail` asserted
// only that some diagnostic containing "dangling" appeared, while `pass`
// asserts it compiles, links, runs, and returns 5 — i.e. that the promoted
// pointer is READABLE after `make` returns, which is exactly the property a
// frame-slot lowering would violate. The refusals for the shapes that really
// do dangle are pinned separately (tests/logos/fail/bc_slice_return_*_fail,
// tests/logos/fail/bc_return_borrow_of_temp_projection_fail), with their
// admit twin at tests/logos/pass/bc_const_promotion_admit.
//
// Original note (kept for provenance): "Returning a reference to a temporary
// literal — the temp dies at end of statement; the returned reference
// dangles. Previously a known gap; closed in B75 by recursing prov_of through
// AddrOfTemp to detect literal / fresh-allocation / call-result inner exprs"
package nll_return_ref_to_temp;

fn make() -> &i32 {
    return &5i32;
}

fn main() -> i32 { return *make(); }
